==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰer-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|referren}}, from {{der|en|fro|referer}}, from {{der|en|la|referre}}. The noun (used in journalism) is from the verb. {{doublet|en|relate}}. See also [[infer]], [[collate]] and [[confer]], [[delate]] and [[defer]], as well as [[prelate]] and [[prefer]] among others.

===Pronunciation===
'''Verb'''
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈfɜː/|a=RP,AU}}
* {{enPR|rī-fûr|a=GA,CA}}, {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈfɝ/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-refer.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈfɛr/|a=Scotland}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɹəˈføː/|a=NZ}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈfeː/|a=Scouse,square-nurse}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈfɛː/|a=Humberside,Teesside,square-nurse}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈfɜː(ɹ)/|a=Lancashire,square-nurse}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɜː(ɹ)|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|re|fer}}
'''Noun'''
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹiːfə/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹifɚ/|a=GA,CA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹiːfə/|a=AU,NZ}}
** {{audio|en|En-au-reefer.ogg|a=Queensland}}
* {{homophone|en|reefer}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːfə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|++}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To direct the [[attention]] of (someone toward something).
#: {{uxi|en|The shop assistant '''referred''' me to the help desk on ground floor.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[submit]] to (another [[person]] or [[group]]) for [[consideration]]; to send or direct elsewhere.
#: {{syn|en|send}}
#: {{uxi|en|He '''referred''' the matter to the principal.}}
#: {{uxi|en|The doctor may '''refer''' patients to a psychiatrist.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'll '''refer''' the objections back to management for reconsideration.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To place in or under by a mental or rational process; to assign to, as a class, a cause, source, a motive, reason, or ground of explanation.
#: {{uxi|en|He '''referred''' the phenomena to electrical disturbances.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1918|author=James George Frazer|title=Folk-Lore In The Old Testament|volume=1|page=54|text=The Nandi of British East Africa tell a story in which the origin of death is '''referred''' to the ill-humour of a dog, who brought the tidings of immortality to men, but, not being received with the deference due to so august an embassy, he changed his tune in a huff and doomed mankind to the sad fate to which they have ever since been subject.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[mention]] (something); to direct [[attention]] (to something).
#: {{ux|en|To explain the problem, the teacher '''referred''' to an example in another textbook.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1854|title=Reports of Cases Argued and Determined in the Supreme Court of Louisiana|page=444
|passage=Her counsel has complained of the charge of the judge, as unprecedented, an innovation on our rules of practice, misconstructive of the true meaning of the law, and '''referring''' to laws not applicable to the sale.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1956|month=February|author=R. C. Riley|title=The Lymington Railway and Isle of Wight Ferry|journal=Railway Magazine|page=82|text=She became disrespectfully '''referred''' to locally as the "Crab" on account of her extraordinary manoeuvrability.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1994|author=Congress of the United States|title=[[s: Page:United_States_Statutes_at_Large_Volume_108_Part_4.djvu/746|United States Statutes at Large]]|volume=108|others=part 4|passage=A provision of law may not be construed as requiring a new grant to be awarded to a specified non-Federal Government entity unless that provision of law (1) specifically '''refers''' to this subsection; specifically identifies the particular non-Federal Government entity involved; and (3) specifically states that the award to that entity is required by such provision of law in contravention of the policy set forth in subsection (a).}}
#* {{quote-video game|en|developer=w:BioWare|title={{w|Mass Effect (video game)|Mass Effect}}|location=Redwood City|publisher=Electronic Arts|year=2008|system=PC|scene=Hanar Codex entry|isbn=9780784546642|oclc=246633669|passage=All hanar have two names. The Face Name is known to the world; the Soul Name is kept for use among close friends and relations. Hanar never '''refer''' to themselves in the first person in conversation with someone they know on a Face Name basis. To do so is considered egotistical, so instead they '''refer''' to themselves as "this one", or the impersonal "it".}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=2009-07-31|author=Bruce Weber|title=Gayatri Devi, 90, a Maharani and a Lawmaker, Dies|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2009/07/31/world/asia/31devi.html|passage=Nonetheless, for many years afterward, she was often '''referred''' to as maharani, though that eventually gave way to the less glamorous but more respectful title of rajmata, the equivalent of queen mother.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251003050815/https://www.nytimes.com/2009/07/31/world/asia/31devi.html|archivedate=3 October 2025}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|stative}} To make [[reference]] to; to be [[about]]; to [[relate]] to; to [[regard]]; to [[allude]] to.
#: {{ux|en|The recipe '''referred''' to several unusual ingredients.}}
# {{lb|en|grammar}} To be [[referential]] to another element in a sentence. <!--refer TO??-->
#: {{rfex|en}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|programming}} To [[point]] to either a specific [[location]] in computer [[memory]] or to a specific [[object]]. {{+obj|en|:to}}
#: {{uxi|en|In C, the pointer obtained by <code>&a</code> '''refers''' to the variable <code>a</code>.}}
#* {{quote-book|en
|url=https://books.google.ch/books?id=rC6la__FTU4C&pg=PA201&dq=%22refers%22
|title=Programming In C: A Practical Approach
|author=Ajay Mittal
|publisher=Pearson Education India
|isbn=9788131729342|oclc=1047790524|date=2010-09|page=201
|passage=Hence, the precise number of bytes to which the pointer '''refers''' to is not known. The compiler must know the number of bytes to which a pointer '''refers''' to in order to apply dereference operation
}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|education}} To [[require]] to [[resit]] an [[examination]].
#: {{ux|en|Smith's marks in the finals were unsatisfactory and he was '''referred'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of a term or name}} To have the meaning of, to [[denote]].
#: {{ux|en|The nickname "Big Apple" '''refers''' to the city of New York.}}
#: {{ux|en|In programming, a "memory leak" '''refers''' to a situation where memory is or stays unnecessarily allocated.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=William F. Basener|title=Topology and Its Applications|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=LKkXXx0VYPMC&pg=PT26&dq=%22bijection%22%7C%22bijections%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=Ug7dUrfpGc3jkgWAt4CYBA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22bijection%22%7C%22bijections%22&f=false|page=unnumbered
|passage=The basic idea is that two sets A and B have '''the same cardinality''' if there is a bijection from A to B. Since the domain and range of the bijection is not relevant here, we often '''refer''' to a bijection from A to B as a '''bijection between the sets''', or a '''one-to-one correspondence''' between the elements of the sets.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine|transitive}} To cause (pain) to be felt elsewhere.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021|author=Ravikiran Ongole; et al|title=Textbook of Oral Medicine, Oral Diagnosis and Oral Radiology|page=206
|passage=Inferior head '''refers''' pain to the TMJ area and the superior head '''refers''' pain to the zygomatic area.}}
#*: The gluteus medius and minimus muscles are important hip abductors {{...}} TrPs in these muscles may produce local pain [...] or may '''refer''' pain down the posterior or lateral thigh and leg [...] 
# {{lb|en|medicine|of the sensation of pain}} To apparently relocate; to be felt in another place than where it is actually caused.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Donald E. Greydanus; Arthur N. Feinberg; Dilip R. Patel|title=The Pediatric Diagnostic Examination|page=316
|passage=Pain from the hip often '''refers''' to the knee.}}

